# Code owners for local-operator-ui.
#
# THERE ARE NO PATTERNS IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. No line here starts
# with a path or `*`; a matching pattern is the only thing that makes GitHub
# create a review request, so nothing is requested automatically when a PR opens.
#
# 2026-09-16 -- AUTO-REQUESTS REMOVED (operator decision). The `*` line that
# used to sit at the bottom of this file (`@damianvtran @bbqben`) existed only to
# route: GitHub requests every handle on a pattern matching a pull request's
# changed files, automatically, at the moment the PR opens -- so `bbqben` was
# notified on every change in this repository, including the ones still churning
# through review rounds and QA. There is no GitHub setting that keeps the routing
# list and drops the notification; matching a pattern IS the request. The pattern
# is gone, and this path is kept, pattern-free, as the record of the change.
#
# 2026-09-22 -- THE ENFORCEMENT HALF ARRIVED, AND IT DID NOT PUT A PATTERN BACK.
# The default branch is now governed by a ruleset named `Main Protection`,
# mirroring the backend repository's (`local-operator`, ruleset 3622629): a
# `non_fast_forward` rule, plus a `pull_request` rule with
# `required_approving_review_count: 1`, `require_code_owner_review: false`,
# `required_review_thread_resolution: false`, `dismiss_stale_reviews_on_push:
# false`, `require_last_push_approval: false`,
# `require_extra_approval_for_unattributed_changes: true`, and
# `allowed_merge_methods: [merge, squash, rebase]`; plus a bypass actor of
# `RepositoryRole` id 5 (admin) with `bypass_mode: always`. Diff those sentences
# against the repository rather than trusting them:
#
#   gh api repos/damianvtran/local-operator-ui/rulesets
#   gh api repos/damianvtran/local-operator-ui/rules/branches/main
#
# `require_code_owner_review: false` is what lets this file stay pattern-free. A
# code-owner rule with no paths declared is an unsatisfiable control -- every
# merge would have to come through the admin bypass, which is the failure this
# file's 2026-09-16 change was made to avoid -- so the rule is off and the
# review *count* is what carries the protection. The count is the tier-2 rule in
# AGENTS.md, "Who may merge: two tiers": an outside contributor's PR cannot reach
# `main` without an approving review from a collaborator other than its author.
# Before 2026-09-22 there was no ruleset here at all -- `gh api
# repos/<owner>/<repo>/rules/branches/main` answered `[]` -- so nothing mechanical
# stood behind those rules; now the approval count and the no-force-push rule do,
# and the agent-review discipline is unchanged.
#
# The owner set -- whose judgement stands in for the project's, and who may be
# asked for a human review when one is genuinely wanted -- is `damianvtran`
# (admin), `bbqben`, `olafagbemi`, `sherman-tsui` and `SanaKetabchi`; each holds
# the **admin** repository role here as of 2026-09-22, mirroring the backend's
# set exactly. `jcobhams` holds write access but is deliberately NOT in that set:
# ownership here means "can approve on behalf of the project", which is narrower
# than commit access. Verify a handle before relying on it with
# `gh api repos/damianvtran/local-operator-ui/collaborators`, since a handle
# without write access is silently ignored by GitHub -- and note that the review
# count is satisfied by an approving review from *any* collaborator other than
# the author, not only from the five above, because no code owners are declared.
#
# The self-approval limitation is the thing to read before adding a pattern
# back: GitHub refuses `422 Review Can not approve your own pull request`, and
# every agent here pushes as the owner's account, so an agent-authored PR the
# owner opened can never be clicked approved by the account that opened it. That
# is why the admin-role bypass is the *sanctioned* completion for the owner's own
# reviewed work here rather than a hole -- and why it must be disclosed on the PR
# (AGENTS.md, "Who may merge: two tiers"; the backend's section of the same name,
# and its "How the forge records tier 1", are the long form).
#
# When a human review IS wanted it is a deliberate act rather than a side effect
# of opening a PR: add the reviewer by hand at the point the PR is ready to be
# read, `gh pr edit <n> --add-reviewer <handle>` (no leading `@` -- gh <= 2.100
# only strips it for `@me` and `@copilot`). Tagging a person in a *comment* is
# what says the PR is waiting on them, so neither is done casually.
#
# Validate any future edit with GitHub's own parser rather than by eye, and pin
# the ref or it silently validates the default branch instead of your branch:
# `gh api 'repos/damianvtran/local-operator-ui/codeowners/errors?ref=<branch>'`
# (an empty error list means the file parsed).
